From 227bd49ba3d486e913993f9d42deb651928c2d02 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 3 Jul 2026 11:30:13 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 05549/11362] [outer-loop] exclusion_manifest_lint:
 auto-downgrade prose-addressed scope-matches

User: 'how do we prevent this from getting stuck again?' after the fourth
milestone-activation stall this session (.471, .474, .475-transient, .476).

The recurring shape (most cleanly demonstrated by .474's 7-task, ~45-minute
stall): the planner's OWN task prompt already explains why a scope-match to
a prior isn't a doomed rerun -- but that reasoning lives in unstructured
prose, and the lint only recognizes a structured prior_failures: block or
operator_override: field. The information is present; it's just in the
wrong place. .476's WRONG_MECHANISM_PRECONDITION stall self-resolved before
it could be diagnosed (the conductor's own stuck-roadmap-clears-and-replans
fallback, same as .475), so this fix targets the pattern demonstrated
conclusively by .474 rather than .476's specific (unrecoverable) violation.

Added _prose_addresses_prior(): when CLASS 3 (SCOPE_MATCHED_PRIOR_FAILURE)
finds a scope-match with no valid structured prior_failures: block, check
whether the task's prompt names the matched prior's experiment_id VERBATIM
with differentiation language (hardens/closes/was blocked/different from/
etc) within a wide window around that mention. If so, auto-downgrade
HARD -> WARNING -- same downgrade-only semantics as operator_override,
never a full clear. The detail field gets a prepended AUTO-DOWNGRADED
marker (prepended, not appended, since _format_risks truncates detail to
200 chars at print time and an appended marker would silently vanish
whenever check.reason is itself near the cap) so every auto-downgrade
stays visible in the activation report for later audit -- this is a safety
net for a demonstrated planner failure mode, not a lowered bar, and the
planner should still be pushed toward populating the structured field.

Deliberately conservative to avoid becoming a new loophole: requires the
EXACT prior id string (case-insensitive), not a fuzzy scope match, so a
coincidental unrelated 'different' elsewhere in a long prompt can't
suppress a genuine violation -- same design principle as the existing
_is_negated_context negation-window guard this reuses the shape of.

4 new tests (TestScopeMatchedPriorFailureProseAutoDowngrade, a new fixture
seeding results/ with a real prior-failure artifact so CLASS 3's actual
ledger-matching logic fires, not a mock): baseline confirms the fixture
triggers a genuine HARD block; prose naming the prior with differentiation
language downgrades to WARNING with the audit marker; prose merely
mentioning the prior id WITHOUT differentiation language stays HARD (the
marker phrase is load-bearing, not just the id); a genuine structured
prior_failures: block still fully clears as before (auto-downgrade is a
fallback, not a replacement). Full suite (71 tests across this file +
in_process_doc_reconcile) green.

Documented in CLAUDE.md's Exclusion-Manifest Cross-Check mechanical-
enforcement paragraph.

